    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. How the Numbers Actually Compare\n\nAt first glance, comparing annualized averages makes the offers look close ($552,000 vs. $526,000), but Google\u2019s front-loaded equity structure changes the math significantly.\n\nGoogle standard new-hire grants vest **33% in Year 1, 33% in Year 2, 22% in Year 3, and 12% in Year 4**:\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google L6 (Year 1 & 2) | Google L6 (Year 3) | Google L6 (Year 4) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) |\n| **Equity Vesting** | ~$250,000 | **$356,400** (33%) | **$237,600** (22%) | **$129,600** (12%) |\n| **Total Comp (TC)** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400** | **~$519,600** *(+ refresh)* | **~$411,600** *(+ refresh)* |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n* **Years 1 & 2 are a solid bump:** You would make roughly **+$112,000/yr (+21%)** more at Google for the first two years.\n* **The Year 3\u20134 \"Equity Cliff\":** Google uses front-loading so Year 1 looks appealing. While annual refreshers (which vest 25%/yr) stack over time to offset this drop, performance refreshers are not guaranteed.\n* **Base Salary is lower:** Your base salary actually decreases by $5,000, despite the top of their posted range reaching $271,000.\n* **No Sign-on Bonus:** Leaving mid-cycle or walking away from unvested equity usually warrants a sign-on bonus.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is This a Good Offer?\n\n* **Market Benchmark:** For a Google L6 Senior PM in the Bay Area, the median total compensation is around **$510,000\u2013$530,000 annualized**. At **$552,000 annualized ($1.08M stock)**, this initial offer is in the **65th\u201375th percentile** of standard initial offers.\n* **For Your Situation:** It is a respectable opening offer, but **not compelling enough to leave a role where you are happy and thriving without negotiating.**\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommendation: Negotiate (Do Not Accept As-Is)\n\nYou hold **maximum leverage**:\n1. You were passively recruited.\n2. You are content at Meta.\n3. You have substantial unvested equity ($200k+ in the next 12 months alone).\n\nGoogle expects L6 candidates to counter.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\n| Component | Offer | Target Ask | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | You currently make $240,000 base. You shouldn't take a base pay cut, and the posted range tops out at $271,000. |\n| **RSUs (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | L6 top-of-band equity can stretch to ~$1.25M\u2013$1.3M+ for strong hires. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$60,000 \u2013 $75,000** | Offsets leaving your current unvested equity and performance cycle at Meta. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nContact your recruiter over a quick call (or via email) and anchor around your current trajectory and enthusiasm for the team.\n\n#### Example Talking Points:\n> *\"I really enjoyed meeting the team, and the scope of the problem space in SF is compelling. However, since I was not actively looking and am performing well at Meta with strong unvested equity, compensation is going to be the deciding factor for me to make a move.*\n> \n> *Looking at the numbers:*\n> 1. *My current base is $240,000, so $235,000 would be a step back on guaranteed cash. Given the posted range goes up to $271,000, I'd like to see the base salary at **$255,000**.*\n> 2. *To offset what I\u2019m walking away from at Meta and ensure the long-term package is competitive into Years 3 and 4, I\u2019d like to see the equity grant at **$1.25M**.*\n> 3. *I'd also need a **$60,000 sign-on bonus** to bridge the transition.*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign and begin planning my start date.\"*\n\n#### How Google will likely respond:\n* **Base Salary:** They will likely meet you in the middle around **$245,000\u2013$250,000**.\n* **Equity:** They may bump the grant to **$1.15M\u2013$1.2M**.\n* **Sign-on:** This is often the easiest lever for Google to pull when they hit band limits on equity or base; expect them to offer **$40,000\u2013$60,000**.",
