    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Breaking Down the New Math\n\nA **$200,000 sign-on bonus** at L6 is an exceptional win. Sign-on bonuses above $100k are rare at Google and typically reserved for Director-level hires or extreme unvested equity matches.\n\nAssuming standard Google front-loaded equity (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%):\n\n| Component | Meta (Status Quo) | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 | Google Year 3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | $48,000 (20%) | $48,000 (20%) |\n| **Equity Vesting** | ~$250,000 | $356,400 | $356,400 | $237,600 *(+ refreshes)* |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$200,000** | $0 | $0 |\n| **Total Comp (TC)** | **~$526,000** | **~$844,400** | **~$644,400** | **~$525,600** *(+ refreshes)* |\n\n#### The Financial Bottom Line:\n* **Year 1:** You make **+$318,000 (+60%)** compared to staying at Meta.\n* **First 2 Years Combined:** You will pull in **~$1.49M** at Google vs. **~$1.05M** at Meta\u2014a net gain of roughly **+$440,000** over 24 months.\n* **Risk Mitigated:** The $200k cash sign-on completely neutralizes the risk of walking away from your upcoming Meta cliff. It effectively guarantees your upside in liquid cash upfront rather than stock volatility.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is This Genuinely Their \"Best Offer\"?\n\n**Yes.** \n* A $200,000 sign-on bonus alongside $1.08M in stock puts your Year 1 package well above the 90th percentile for an L6 PM. \n* Compensation committees rarely approve cash sign-ons of this magnitude for IC roles without exhausting the rest of their standard band exceptions. Pushing further on comp is unlikely to yield more and risks souring the relationship before you start.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Non-Financial Factors to Weigh\n\nBecause the compensation is now unequivocally in Google\u2019s favor for at least the next 2\u20133 years, your decision comes down to team, culture, and career trajectory:\n\n#### Reasons to Take It:\n1. **Financial Arbitrage:** Bank ~$440k+ in extra earnings over the next two years.\n2. **Product & Resume Diversity:** Having both Meta (fast-paced, execution-heavy, bottom-up) and Google (platform-scale, consensus-driven) at Senior/Staff PM levels makes you highly marketable for future VP/CPO roles.\n3. **Pacing / Work-Life Balance:** Meta\u2019s performance culture (PSC) has historically been more demanding than Google's, though expectations vary by org.\n\n#### Reasons to Walk Away (or Stay at Meta):\n1. **Product Execution Velocity:** If you thrive on Meta\u2019s shipping speed and autonomous decision-making, Google\u2019s consensus-heavy culture and matrixed approvals can sometimes feel frustrating for incoming PMs.\n2. **Path to L7 / IC7:** If you are within 6\u201312 months of an IC7 promotion at Meta with strong executive sponsorship, walking away resets your promotion clock. (However, Google L6 to L7 is also a well-trodden path).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Verdict & Next Steps\n\n**Verdict: Accept the offer.** \n\nYou extracted maximum value from the negotiation. You held your base salary, secured top-tier equity, and added $200k in guaranteed cash. \n\n#### Before You Sign, Clarify These Two Details:\n1. **Sign-on Payout & Clawback Terms:** Check whether the $200k is paid in your first paycheck or split across Month 1 and Month 12, and confirm the clawback period (typically 12 months pro-rated, occasionally 24 months).\n2. **Start Date vs. Meta Vesting:** Time your start date strategically to capture any imminent Meta quarterly RSU vest if it falls within the next 30\u201345 days.",
